@startuml
' Figure 6.3 – System sequence diagram: preview unlock then full purchase
!include _style.puml
mainframe interaction SequenceDiagram Two-stage article purchase
autonumber "0 :"

participant "Magazine" as M
participant "System" as A
participant "Database" as D

ref over M, D : Sign in

M -> A : POST /purchases/preview with an idempotency key
activate A
A -> A : Check the account type, the active subscription,\nthe placement and that the buyer is not the author

group One database transaction
  A -> D : SELECT the credit balance FOR UPDATE
  activate D
  D --> A : Locked balance
  alt balance below the preview price
    A --> M : 402, offer to top up
  else balance sufficient
    A -> D : Debit the magazine by 10% of the price
    A -> D : Credit the writer with the payout
    A -> D : Insert the purchase row, stage preview_unlock
    A -> D : Insert three transaction rows\n(debit, writer payout, platform fee)
    D --> A : Committed
  end
  deactivate D
end
A --> M : Preview unlocked, new balance
deactivate A
M -> A : Read the full article

|||

M -> A : POST /purchases/buy with an idempotency key
activate A
A -> D : Look up the prior preview for this article and magazine
activate D
D --> A : Preview row, or none
deactivate D
A -> A : Remaining = price minus what the preview already paid

group One database transaction
  A -> D : SELECT the credit balance FOR UPDATE
  activate D
  A -> D : Debit the remaining amount, credit the writer
  A -> D : Insert the purchase row, stage full_purchase,\nlinked to the preview
  A -> D : Insert three transaction rows
  D --> A : Committed
  deactivate D
end
A --> M : Republish rights granted, article added to the library
deactivate A

note over M, D
  Measured on a 100-credit article: 445 to 435 for the preview,
  then 435 to 345 for the remainder. Ledger rows: 10 = 8 payout
  + 2 fee, then 90 = 72 payout + 18 fee. The row lock is what
  stops two concurrent purchases spending the same credits.
end note
@enduml
